# Scientific Basis for Reconstruction and Chassis Validation

This document records the scientific ideas used by the suite. It does not convert these studies into claims that a formula model can predict sensory equivalence.

## 1. Odor mixtures are nonlinear

At suprathreshold concentrations, mixture intensity commonly shows suppression or hypoadditivity rather than simple arithmetic addition. Peri-threshold and subthreshold components can still change mixture quality. This supports omission testing and warns against deleting every row with an isolated OAV below one.

1. V. Ferreira (2012), *Flavour and Fragrance Journal*, 27, 124-140, 127 citations.  
   “Revisiting psychophysical work on the quantitative and qualitative odour properties of simple odour mixtures: a flavour chemistry view. Part 1: intensity and detectability.”  
   https://consensus.app/papers/revisiting-psychophysical-work-on-the-quantitative-and-ferreira/fbb50bd17b4d592cbd5e1f753f7cf083/?utm_source=chatgpt

2. Vijay Singh, Nicolle R. Murphy, V. Balasubramanian, J. Mainland (2018/2019), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*, 116, 9598-9603, 73 citations.  
   “Competitive binding predicts nonlinear responses of olfactory receptors to complex mixtures.”  
   https://consensus.app/papers/competitive-binding-predicts-nonlinear-responses-of-singh-murphy/8d20b2ac2eda59749b6daf16b83fe3a9/?utm_source=chatgpt

3. T. Thomas-Danguin et al. (2014), *Frontiers in Psychology*, 5, 227 citations.  
   “The perception of odor objects in everyday life: a review on the processing of odor mixtures.”  
   https://consensus.app/papers/the-perception-of-odor-objects-in-everyday-life-a-review-on-thomas-danguin-sinding/c9470ac46b485864b671021295931fbb/?utm_source=chatgpt

## 2. Time-resolved headspace matters

HS-SPME-GC-MS can characterize fragrance-derived volatiles and temporal change on skin, but skin release differs among participants. This supports several timepoints and prevents a single headspace measurement from being treated as the formula.

4. E. Duffy, Gwendoline Albero, A. Morrin (2018), *Cosmetics*, 28 citations.  
   “Headspace Solid-Phase Microextraction Gas Chromatography-Mass Spectrometry Analysis of Scent Profiles from Human Skin.”  
   https://consensus.app/papers/headspace-solidphase-microextraction-gas-duffy-albero/907412b2c1885854bdbbdf9fc39317d3/?utm_source=chatgpt

## 3. Matrix changes release

Different bases can retain or release fragrance components differently. A correct concentrate placed into a different vehicle may have a different headspace and sensory profile.

5. Lucrezia Di Nicolantonio et al. (2022), *Cosmetics*, 10 citations.  
   “Impact of the Interactions between Fragrances and Cosmetic Bases on the Fragrance Olfactory Performance: A Tentative to Correlate SPME-GC/MS Analysis with That of an Experienced Perfumer.”  
   https://consensus.app/papers/impact-of-the-interactions-between-fragrances-and-nicolantonio-gigliobianco/516fdbbce0905e8390d74170f897e1ef/?utm_source=chatgpt

## 4. Operational implications

The suite therefore requires:

- target, stock, and finished-matrix accounting;
- several timepoints;
- controlled substrate and dose;
- omission and addition tests;
- no assumption that rank, peak area, or isolated OAV equals perceptual dominance;
- explicit uncertainty;
- and sensory validation of every alternative module.

These references support the workflow principles. They do not authenticate any case-study formula in this archive.
